    "display_name": "Theater Tickets",
    "long_instructions": "Now that you’ve built a prediction model, you’d like to use it in a program that lets users say how far they want to be from the stage and get an estimated cost.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. Prompt the user to type in how many feet they want to be from the front of the stage, then save that integer in a variable.\r\n2. Use the variable to calculate a predicted cost for the user’s ticket.\r\n3. Print a message letting the user know the distance from the stage and the predicted cost.\r\n\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nIf you’re asking the user for input and you want Python to treat that input as a number, you’ll usually want to convert the input to an `int` first. You can use code like this to convert the input:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nint(input(\"Type in a number: \"))\r\n```",
